The `HttpRequestHandler` class is designed to handle HTTP requests and responses, specifically for sending POST requests.

**Writing a POST Request**

To write a POST request, you can use the following code:
```java
public void writePost(Connection.KeyVal keyVal, OutputStream outputStream) throws IOException {
    // ...

    String boundary = null;
    // ...

    if (boundary != null) {
        for (Connection.KeyVal keyVal : data) {
            w.write("--");
            w.write(boundary);
            w.write("\r\n");
            w.write("Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"");
            w.write(encodeMimeName(keyVal.key()));
            w.write("\"");
            final InputStream input = keyVal.inputStream();
            if (input != null) {
                w.write("; filename=\"");
                w.write(encodeMimeName(keyVal.value()));
                w.write("\"\r\nContent-Type: ");
                String contentType = keyVal.contentType();
                w.write(contentType != null ? contentType : DefaultUploadType);
                w.write("\r\n\r\n");
                w.flush(); // flush
                DataUtil.crossStreams(input, outputStream);
                outputStream.flush();
            } else {
                w.write("\r\n\r\n");
                w.write(keyVal.value());
            }
            w.write("\r\n");
        }
        w.write("--");
        w.write(boundary);
        w.write("--");
    } else {
        String body = req.requestBody();
        if (body != null) {
            // data will be in query string, we're sending a plaintext body
            w.write(body);
        } else {
            // regular form data (application/x-www-form-urlencoded)
            boolean first = true;
            for (Connection.KeyVal keyVal : data) {
                if (!first) {
                    w.append('&');
                } else {
                    first = false;
                }
                w.write(URLEncoder.encode(keyVal.key(), req.postDataCharset()));
                w.write('=');
                w.write(URLEncoder.encode(keyVal.value(), req.postDataCharset()));
            }
        }
    }

    w.close();
}
```
**Serializing a POST Request**

To serialize a POST request, you can use the following code:
```java
public static void serialiseRequestUrl(Connection.Request req) throws IOException {
    // ...

    for (Connection.KeyVal keyVal : req.data()) {
        Validate.isFalse(keyVal.hasInputStream(), "InputStream data not supported in URL query string.");
        in.appendKeyVal(keyVal);
    }
    req.url(null); // remove the URL from the request
    req.data().clear(); // move data into URL as get params

    // ...
}
```
**Writing a POST Request with Data**

To write a POST request with data, you can use the `writePost` method and pass in a `KeyVal` object:
```java
public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    Connection.Request req = new Connection.Request("http://example.com", "POST");
    KeyVal key1 = new KeyVal("key1", "value1");
    KeyVal key2 = new KeyVal("key2", "value2");

    req.data().put(key1);
    req.data().put(key2);

    OutputStream outputStream = System.out;

    handler.writePost(req, outputStream);

    Serialiser.serialiseRequestUrl(req);
}
```
This will send a POST request with the specified data.

**Example Use Case**

Here's an example use case:
```java
public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    Connection.Request req = new Connection.Request("http://example.com", "POST");
    KeyVal key1 = new KeyVal("key1", "value1");
    KeyVal key2 = new KeyVal("key2", "value2");

    req.data().put(key1);
    req.data().put(key2);

    OutputStream outputStream = System.out;

    handler.writePost(req, outputStream);

    Serialiser.serialiseRequestUrl(req);
}
```
This will send a POST request with the specified data and print it to the console.

**Notes**

* The `writePost` method can be used to write any type of data, including strings, integers, booleans, etc.
* The `serialiseRequestUrl` method is used to serialize a POST request, but it's not recommended to use this method unless you're sending sensitive data.
* The `KeyVal` class has a `hasInputStream` method that returns whether the specified key contains an input stream. This is used to determine whether to send a file or not.

